      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2011 The Authors. Respirology © 2011 Asian Pacific Society of Respirology.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "The risk of pneumonia is increased among COPD patients using inhaled corticosteroids (ICS). However, there is uncertainty regarding the association between long-term use of ICS and exacerbations of respiratory tract infections among asthmatic patients."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "A case-control nested cohort study was performed to assess the association of asthma with nontuberculous mycobacterium (NTM) infection."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Among this cohort of 464 asthmatic patients, 14 experienced complications due to NTM infections, of which eight were caused by Mycobacterium avium-intracellulare complex, three by M.\u2003kansasii, one by M.terrae and the remaining two by unclassifiable scotochromogens. Asthmatic patients with NTM infections were older (67.1 ± 8.6 vs 58.8 ± 12.3 years, P\u2003<\u20030.01) and had more severe airflow limitation (FEV(1) %, 60.6 ± 10.3 vs 72.3 ± 18.3, P\u2003<\u20030.03) than those without NTM infections. All except one had received ICS treatment for more than 5 years, and 12 of the 14 patients used inhaled fluticasone propionate daily (four patients at a dose of 400 µg/day and eight patients at a dose >800 µg/day)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "These findings suggest that the risk of NTM infection may be greater in asthmatic patients who are older, have more severe airflow limitation and receive higher doses of ICS therapy."
          }
        ]
      },
